WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.368
Java's own streams are built in layers.

2
00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:07.214
Start with the real stream.

3
00:00:07.214 --> 00:00:12.960
Wrap it in a GZIPOutputStream, then a BufferedOutputStream, then a DataOutputStream.

4
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.374
Each wrapper is an OutputStream too.

5
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:19.360
Each one adds one behavior: typed writes, buffering, compression.

6
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.488
Then it passes the bytes on to the stream it wraps.

7
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:24.560
That is the decorator pattern.

8
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.219
A decorator has the same interface as the object it wraps.

9
00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:31.185
It does one extra thing, and forwards the call.

10
00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:34.213
The caller can't tell how many layers there are.

11
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:38.920
Fees fit the same shape, because a fee is a base rule plus adjustments.

12
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.189
The base rule is 2.9 percent of the amount.

13
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:44.320
Then each adjustment wraps it.

14
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.244
Add 30 cents.

15
00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:49.081
Add 1 percent more for a payment from outside Germany.

16
00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.071
Then cap the fee at 5 euros.

17
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:54.492
Each call in the chain wraps everything to its left.

18
00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:57.625
So the last one, cappedAt, ends up on the outside.

19
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:00.567
Now charge a French payment of 1,000 euros.

20
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.173
The call goes to the outside layer first.

21
00:01:03.173 --> 00:01:07.432
Each layer calls the one it wraps, all the way in to the base rule.

22
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.191
The base rule returns 29 euros.

23
00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:15.965
On the way back out, each layer does its one thing: 29.30, then 39.30.

24
00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:19.429
The cap comes last, so the fee is 5 euros.

25
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.236
Now move cappedAt in front of crossBorder.

26
00:01:22.236 --> 00:01:24.371
Same layers, in a different order.

27
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.291
Run the same payment.

28
00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:27.499
29, then 29.30.

29
00:01:27.499 --> 00:01:29.755
The cap brings it down to 5.

30
00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:34.909
Then the surcharge lands after the cap, and the fee is 15 euros.

31
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.458
Both chains compile.

32
00:01:36.458 --> 00:01:40.840
Only a test with a large cross-border payment tells them apart.

33
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.380
Behavior decorators have the same issue.

34
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.974
Put retry outside logging, and every attempt is logged.

35
00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:52.091
Swap them, and you log one call, however many attempts it took.

36
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:55.049
Pick the order you want, and pin it in a test.

37
00:01:55.967 --> 00:02:00.096
In Java, each decorator is a default method on the interface.

38
00:02:00.096 --> 00:02:04.428
It returns a new lambda, and that lambda is a FeeCalculator too.

39
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:07.919
The lambda calls feeMinor on the calculator it wraps.

40
00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.833
Then it does its one extra thing, here the cap.

41
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:15.551
In Kotlin, the decorator is an extension function on a fun interface.

42
00:02:15.551 --> 00:02:21.116
Either way, each call returns a new calculator, and the one it wraps stays as it was.
